experiment  bm128_roofline: at BLOCK_M=128, does achieved throughput plateau BELOW
            this card's own measured dense bf16 rate, in the multi-tile regime?

## Registered predictions, with numbers, before anything is measured

  the roof            682.1 TFLOP/s dense bf16, MEASURED on the attached card
  multi-tile onset    T > 512 on mixtral-8x7b (r > 128 rows per expert, from T = r E / k with E=8, k=2)
  deepest point       4096 rows per expert = 32 M-tiles per expert, 5 multi-tile points

  P1  the control BLOCK_M=256 beats the subject by at least 0.10 of the roof
      (68 TFLOP/s). Its cap is 89.7 Op/B against the subject's 75.1 and a ridge of 155.9: 0.58x, so it has headroom on the subject and
      still a ceiling of its own at 0.58x the ridge, under the 1.30x a control with none would need. P5 below is registered to FAIL on that arithmetic alone.
  P2  the subject plateaus between 0.805 and 1.000 of the roof (549 to 682 TFLOP/s), which is what the
      study's own alpha band at BLOCK_M=128 predicts (alpha 0.625 to 1.020, cap 125 Op/B at the top). THE BAND SPANS A QUARTER OF THE RANGE
      IT PREDICTS. It is registered so the fit is on the record, and it is compared against nothing.
  P3  the subject's gain falls below 2.0% per doubling over the last 2 doublings, by n=32 M-tiles.
  P4  the control does NOT plateau below the roof over the same span.
  P5  the control REACHES the roof (0.95 of 682.1 = 648 TFLOP/s), which is what makes the subject's
      shortfall a located ceiling rather than a gap of unknown height. REGISTERED AS THE ONE THIS
      STUDY EXPECTS TO FAIL: no tile in the published corpus reaches 0.95 of ridge x bandwidth,
      the BLOCK_M=256 control peaking at 0.48-0.54 of the roof on the H200 and 0.56-0.64 on the A100,
      and this control's own cap is 0.58x the ridge, so the arithmetic registers the same failure the corpus does.

  THE FORK, in three ways. Throughput reaches the roof -> the ceiling is not binding and the study's
  central claim is about a regime that does not occur. It plateaus below the roof and the control
  plateaus with it -> the shortfall is the fused layer's, not the tile's. It plateaus below the roof,
  the control beats it by the separation, and the control reaches the roof -> the ceiling is real and
  binds where production runs; the control beats it and does NOT reach the roof -> a tile-dependent
  gap with the ceiling unlocated. All four are results; only one is the study's.

